<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="51c8710d">&lt;p&gt;
    Identify an initial set of Backlog Items in alignment with stakeholders’ needs. Collaborate with the stakeholders and
    use business knowledge expertise and inputs from the team, typically during a workshop or series of workshops in which:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Product vision is analyzed and refined into&amp;nbsp;Backlog Items&amp;nbsp;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Additional stakeholder needs are captured as new Backlog Items
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Each identified Backlog Item is discussed until common understanding is reached
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    At first, the Backlog Items can remain at a high-level as they will ultimately be further refined. So, focus on
    refining the portion of the Backlog Items that will be worked on during the first sprint.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Then order the Backlog Items based on:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Value provided to the stakeholders&amp;nbsp;- typically the more valuable the Backlog Item, the higher the
        priority/order
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Effort - Items with high value and low effort are typically prioritized
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Identified risks - Backlog Items with high risk&amp;nbsp;or containing experiments designed to mitigate risks may be
        completed early to reduce overall risk
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Dependencies&amp;nbsp;- if dependencies exist between Backlog Items, some otherwise low-value, low-risk work items
        might be moved to the top of the Product Backlog if a more important Backlog Item depends on them
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Once the above mentioned values, risks and dependencies are identified, they should be captured as part of the
    associated Backlog Item.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    A number of techniques may be used for ordering the Backlog Items, including:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;MoSCoW&lt;/strong&gt; - Preliminary prioritization done by grouping all Backlog Items into either&amp;nbsp;a Must
        have, Should have, Could have, or Would have category
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Weighted Shortest Job First (WSJF)&lt;/strong&gt; - ordering technique based on Backlog Items&apos; value,
        criticality, provided risk mitigation, and size
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Buy a Feature&lt;/strong&gt; -&amp;nbsp;approach where stakeholders are given a certain amount of virtual currency to
        spend on each Backlog Item; amounts may vary depending on the relative importance of a stakeholder; once all
        stakeholders have spent all of their allocated currency, order is based on amount of currency spent on each Backlog
        Item
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The ordered list of Backlog Items constitutes the Product Backlog. Once created, the initial Product Backlog represents
    the early state of knowledge about work to be done and should be made visible to the Development team and stakeholders.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
